---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const updated = '13 August 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Terms and disclaimer"
  description="Terms of use for CyberCVE, including accuracy limits, data source attribution, and trademark use."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <h1>Terms and disclaimer</h1>
        <p>Last updated {updated}.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card prose">
      <h2>This is not security advice</h2>
      <p>
        CyberCVE reports published vulnerability data and counts it in various ways. It does
        not tell you whether you are affected by anything, and it is not a substitute for your
        vendor's advisories, your own asset inventory, or professional advice. Decisions about
        patching your systems should be based on the vendor's advisory and your own
        circumstances — not on a count on this site.
      </p>

      <h2>Accuracy, and its limits</h2>
      <p>
        The data here is assembled automatically from public sources and mapped to product
        categories by rules we wrote. Both steps can be wrong. Known and specific limitations
        are documented on the <a href="/methodology">methodology</a> page, including which years
        are covered and which records are deliberately excluded.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>CVE counts are not a product quality ranking.</strong> A vendor that publishes
        thoroughly and assigns CVEs for its own findings will show more CVEs than one that does
        neither. The methodology page explains this at length because it is the single most
        common way this kind of data is misread.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you find an error, please <a href="/feedback">report it</a> — corrections are the
        point, and the fix history is public.
      </p>

      <h2>No warranty</h2>
      <p>
        This site is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or implied,
        including but not limited to warranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular
        purpose, and non-infringement. To the fullest extent permitted by law, the operator is
        not liable for any claim, damages, or other liability arising from your use of, or
        reliance on, this site or its data.
      </p>

      <h2>Data sources and attribution</h2>
      <p>The vulnerability data on this site comes from third parties and remains theirs:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>CVE records</strong> — the <a href="https://www.cve.org/">CVE Program</a>. Use
          is subject to the CVE Program Terms of Use. CVE® is a registered trademark of MITRE.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog</strong> — <a
            href="https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog">CISA</a
          >, a work of the U.S. Government.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>EPSS scores</strong> — courtesy of <a href="https://first.org/epss">FIRST</a>.
          Free to use with citation.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of these organisations endorse this site or have reviewed it. Any error in how
        their data is presented here is ours.
      </p>

      <h2>Trademarks</h2>
      <p>
        Fortinet, Palo Alto Networks, Cisco, Splunk, CyberArk, and all other vendor and product
        names are trademarks of their respective owners. They are used nominatively — that is,
        to identify the products a vulnerability affects. No affiliation with, sponsorship by,
        or endorsement from any of these companies is claimed or implied.
      </p>

      <h2>Using this site's content</h2>
      <p>
        The source code, including the product taxonomy, is open source under the MIT licence —
        see the <a href="https://github.com/historiantech-eng/cybercves">repository</a>. The
        underlying vulnerability data is not ours to license; take it from the sources above
        under their terms. If you cite figures from this site, please link to the page you took
        them from, since the numbers change as the data does.
      </p>

      <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
      <p>
        The site and its JSON API are free to use. Please do not scrape at a rate that degrades
        it for others — there is an <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a> and a JSON API precisely so
        you do not have to. Abusive traffic may be rate-limited or blocked.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="mailto:contact@cybercve.com">contact@cybercve.com</a> · Security reports: <a
          href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
